WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.840
calculators computers and teachers throughout your&nbsp;
life have told you that you can't divide by zero

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.320
but I'm here to tell you that you can

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.360
it's true in the real numbers&nbsp;
division by zero isn't defined

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.320
but neither is taking square root of -1

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.200
and yet mathematicians found a way to do it

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.560
checking all the details might be a bit complex

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.480
but ultimately the idea is just

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.200
"throw in a number whose square is&nbsp;
-1 then follow the normal rules of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.960
algebra to figure out what this&nbsp;
new number system looks like"

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:35.160
if you do this with the square root of -1&nbsp;
you end up with the familiar complex numbers

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.680
they might have some weird properties compared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:40.520
the reals but they're a perfectly&nbsp;
good system of numbers to work with

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.040
can't we just do the same thing&nbsp;
with division by zero? can't we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.280
just throw in an inverse of&nbsp;
zero and see what happens?

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.200
given that you've been told your whole&nbsp;
life that you can't divide by zero

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.280
you might expect that for reasons&nbsp;
you don't fully understand

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:59.600
you can't just throw in an inverse of a number the&nbsp;
same way you can throw in a solution to x^2 = -1

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.080
nope that's not the problem

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.560
the process of throwing in&nbsp;
the inverse of a number or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.000
set of numbers and seeing what&nbsp;
happens is called localization

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.560
and it's no more difficult to do&nbsp;
than throwing in square root of -1

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.720
suppose you started with the integers

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.920
there's no 1/2 in the&nbsp;
integers so let's throw it in

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.200
in order to be able to multiply numbers&nbsp;
as usual we would also need to throw in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.720
all the multiples of 1/2 including negative ones

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:31.240
and multiplying 1/2 with itself means we also&nbsp;
need 1/4 and all the multiples of a fourth

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:32.520
and so on and so forth

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.480
we need to add in anything that's&nbsp;
an integer divided by a power of two

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:37.440
but then we're good to go

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.600
we end up with what are called&nbsp;
the dyadic or binary rationals

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:44.680
which is another weird but&nbsp;
perfectly valid number system

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:50.480
if we also threw in 1/3, 1/4, 1/5, 1/6,&nbsp;
inverses of every positive whole number

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.280
then we end up with all the&nbsp;
rational numbers--ratios of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.600
integers where the denominator is not zero

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.920
this is a pretty standard way&nbsp;
to construct the rationals

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.600
so what about zero inverse?

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.280
well let's do it

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:06.920
let's start with the real numbers&nbsp;
throw in an inverse of zero and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:09.840
just see what kind of number system pops out

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.000
just like 1/2 is the unique&nbsp;
number such that 2 * 1/2 is 1

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.920
our inverse of zero is going to be the unique&nbsp;
number such that 0 * the inverse of 0 is 1

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.760
so let's assume that's true then what?

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:28.480
pretty quickly we see this implies that&nbsp;
1 equals 0 because anything * 0 is 0

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.160
but wait--this is not a contradiction

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.680
of course in the real&nbsp;
numbers 0 is different than 1

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.120
but in the real numbers x^2&nbsp;
can never be negative either

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:43.280
and yet we're perfectly okay in the&nbsp;
complex numbers saying that i^2 is -1

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.720
just because we've discovered an unintuitive&nbsp;
property of this new number system

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.080
that doesn't make it wrong

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.680
let's just continue and see where it takes us

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:59.000
take any number "x" in this system and note that&nbsp;
x is 1 * x because 1 * anything is that thing

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:07.240
but because 1 = 0 this is also 0 * x which&nbsp;
of course is 0 because 0 * anything is 0

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:12.160
thus we've shown in our new number&nbsp;
system that every number equals 0

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.920
there's still no contradiction, no error in logic

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:17.320
we've just invented what's called the Zero Ring

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.760
where 0 is the only number

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:20.946
0 + 0 = 0,

00:03:20.946 --> 00:03:20.956
0 - 0 = 0,

00:03:20.956 --> 00:03:22.240
0 * 0 = 0,

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:27.520
0 / 0 = 0

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.680
and 1 is just another label for 0

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.160
feel free to check that this&nbsp;
number system satisfies all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.000
the normal rules of algebra it really does

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.120
so it's not that you can't divide by zero

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:51.600
it's just that if you allow it then all&nbsp;
numbers are zero and you get what you deserve

